---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const emailHref = "mailto:olga@okad.agency?subject=Creative%20testing%20framework%20%E2%80%94%20tell%20us%20about%20your%20ads";
const calHref = "https://calendly.com/okad_calls/first";
---

<Layout
  title="Creative Testing Framework: How Top Brands Test Ad Creatives | OKAD"
  description="The complete creative testing framework used by top DTC and SaaS brands. Learn isolation testing, multivariate testing, the hook-body-CTA hierarchy, budget allocation, sample sizes for statistical significance, and testing cadence by spend level."
>
  <Header />
  <main>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="pt-20 md:pt-24 pb-10 text-center bg-bg">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[12px] font-medium text-accent bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.18)] rounded-full px-3.5 py-1.5 mb-6">
          Open reference &middot; September 2026
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(28px,5vw,48px)] leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink mb-4">
          Creative Testing Framework: <span class="text-accent">How Top Brands Test Ad Creatives</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-[18px] text-ink-2 max-w-[600px] mx-auto leading-[1.6] mb-8">
          Most brands test creative wrong. They change everything at once, declare winners too early, and waste budget on inconclusive results. This framework shows you how to test systematically &mdash; isolating variables, reaching statistical significance, and building a compounding knowledge base.
        </p>
        <div class="flex gap-3 justify-center flex-wrap mb-8">
          <a href={emailHref} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-accent text-white hover:bg-accent-hover transition-all no-underline">Get a testing audit &rarr;</a>
          <a href={calHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-card text-ink border border-border hover:border-accent hover:text-accent transition-all no-underline">Book a call &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-5 flex-wrap justify-center font-mono text-[12px] text-muted">
          <span>Testing methodology</span>
          <span>Statistical significance</span>
          <span>Hook &rarr; Body &rarr; CTA</span>
          <span>Budget allocation</span>
          <span>Cadence by spend</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How it works grid -->
    <section class="py-8">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <h2 class="font-display text-[22px] font-bold tracking-[-0.02em] text-center mb-5">Why Creative Testing Matters</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3.5">
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F3AF;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Find Winners Faster</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Systematic testing finds winning creatives 2&ndash;3&times; faster than random launches. You learn what works and double down &mdash; instead of guessing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F4CA;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Compound Knowledge</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Every test teaches you something about your audience. Testing builds an asset: a growing database of what hooks, formats, and angles convert.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F4B8;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Stop Wasting Budget</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Brands without a testing framework waste 30&ndash;50% of ad spend on underperforming creative. Structured testing cuts that waste in half.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <section class="py-10 border-t border-b border-border">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-muted-2 mb-4">Contents</div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-1.5">
          {[
            { num: "01", text: "Overview", href: "#overview" },
            { num: "02", text: "Testing Methodologies", href: "#methodologies" },
            { num: "03", text: "The Testing Hierarchy", href: "#hierarchy" },
            { num: "04", text: "Sample Sizes & Significance", href: "#sample-sizes" },
            { num: "05", text: "Budget Allocation", href: "#budget" },
            { num: "06", text: "Testing Cadence by Spend Level", href: "#cadence" },
            { num: "07", text: "The Testing Workflow", href: "#workflow" },
            { num: "08", text: "Common Testing Mistakes", href: "#mistakes" },
            { num: "09", text: "Advanced Testing Tactics", href: "#advanced" },
            { num: "10", text: "FAQ", href: "#faq" },
          ].map((item) => (
            <a href={item.href} class="flex items-center gap-2.5 px-3 py-2 rounded-lg text-[14px] text-ink-2 font-medium hover:bg-bg-alt transition-colors no-underline">
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium text-accent min-w-[22px]">{item.num}</span>
              {item.text}
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 01 Overview -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="overview">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">01 &mdash; Overview</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Creative testing is a system, not a series of guesses</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Most brands test by launching multiple creatives and seeing what sticks &mdash; this is not testing, it&rsquo;s gambling</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Real testing isolates variables, reaches statistical significance, and builds compounding learnings</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The framework: test hooks first, then body, then CTA, then format</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The difference between brands that scale profitably on paid media and those that plateau comes down to one thing: their creative testing system. Not their budget. Not their product. Not even their creative quality. It&rsquo;s their ability to systematically discover what works, understand <em>why</em> it works, and iterate on that knowledge faster than <a href="/ad-fatigue/" class="text-accent hover:underline">ad fatigue</a> decays their existing winners.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Most brands approach creative testing backwards. They launch 5&ndash;10 ads that are completely different from each other &mdash; different hooks, different messages, different formats, different creators. When one outperforms, they declare it the &ldquo;winner&rdquo; and scale it. But they haven&rsquo;t learned <em>anything</em>. Was it the hook that won? The format? The creator&rsquo;s delivery? The color of their shirt? With everything different, you can&rsquo;t attribute the win to any specific element.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A proper creative testing framework isolates variables, runs tests to statistical significance, and systematically climbs the optimization ladder: hook &rarr; body &rarr; CTA &rarr; format. Each test teaches you something specific. Over weeks and months, these learnings compound into a deep understanding of what your audience responds to &mdash; and that knowledge becomes your most valuable strategic asset.</p>
        <ul class="list-none p-0 space-y-2">
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Goal:</strong> find winning creatives faster while building reusable audience insights</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Method:</strong> isolate variables, test sequentially, reach statistical significance</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Hierarchy:</strong> hook (highest impact) &rarr; body &rarr; CTA &rarr; format</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Budget:</strong> 20&ndash;30% of ad spend allocated to testing</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 Testing Methodologies -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="methodologies">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">02 &mdash; Methodologies</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Three testing methodologies compared</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Isolation testing: change one variable at a time &mdash; the gold standard</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Multivariate testing: test multiple variables simultaneously &mdash; faster but harder to read</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Sequential testing: run concepts in waves &mdash; practical for smaller budgets</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Isolation testing (A/B testing)</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>What it is:</strong> You take a base creative and change exactly one variable &mdash; the hook, the CTA text, the background music, or the creator. Everything else stays identical. You run both versions to the same audience at the same budget and compare results.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>When to use:</strong> When you need clear, attributable insights. Isolation testing is the gold standard for learning <em>why</em> something works. Use it when optimizing proven concepts &mdash; you already have a winning ad and want to make it better.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Limitation:</strong> Slow. You can only test one variable per cycle (3&ndash;7 days). At one variable per week, you might test 50 variables per year &mdash; which is enough for optimization but not for discovery.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Multivariate testing</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>What it is:</strong> You test multiple variables simultaneously by running many different creative combinations. For example, 4 hooks &times; 3 bodies &times; 2 CTAs = 24 ad variants running at the same time.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>When to use:</strong> When you have enough budget to give each variant sufficient impressions and you need to explore a large creative space quickly. Best for brands spending $50K+/month on ads. Requires larger sample sizes per variant to reach significance.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Limitation:</strong> Requires significant budget and sophisticated analysis. With 24 variants, each one needs enough conversions to be statistically meaningful. At $10 CPA and a 95% confidence target, you might need $5,000&ndash;$10,000 in test spend alone.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Sequential testing</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>What it is:</strong> You test in waves. Week 1: launch 5 completely different concepts. Identify the top 2 performers. Week 2: create 5 variations of those 2 winners (new hooks, new CTAs). Identify the best variant. Week 3: optimize the winning variant further. Each wave narrows the field.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>When to use:</strong> Best for smaller budgets ($10K&ndash;$50K/month) where you can&rsquo;t afford to run many variants simultaneously. Sequential testing is less rigorous than isolation testing but more practical &mdash; it balances learning speed with budget constraints.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Method</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Best for</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Min budget</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Learning speed</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Rigor</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink font-semibold">Isolation (A/B)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Optimizing winners</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">$5K/month</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Slow</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2 font-semibold" style="color: #2e7d32">Highest</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink font-semibold">Multivariate</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Exploring creative space</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">$50K/month</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Fast</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2 font-semibold" style="color: #f57f17">Medium</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink font-semibold">Sequential</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Smaller budgets</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">$10K/month</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Medium</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2 font-semibold" style="color: #f57f17">Medium</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 Testing Hierarchy -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="hierarchy">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">03 &mdash; Hierarchy</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">The testing hierarchy: what to test first</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Hook &rarr; Body &rarr; CTA &rarr; Format (ordered by impact on performance)</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The hook determines 60&ndash;80% of an ad&rsquo;s performance</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Never test CTA before you&rsquo;ve optimized the hook</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Not all creative elements are equal. The hook (first 1&ndash;3 seconds) has the largest impact on whether your ad succeeds or fails. If people don&rsquo;t stop scrolling, nothing else matters. A brilliant CTA on an ad nobody watches is worthless. Test in order of impact.</p>

        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Hook (first 1&ndash;3 seconds).</strong> Impact: 60&ndash;80% of total performance. The hook determines whether anyone watches your ad. Test hook types (question, statement, visual disruption, problem callout), hook copy, and opening visuals. Your <a href="/hook-rate/" class="text-accent hover:underline">hook rate</a> (3-second retention) is the primary metric. Run 5&ndash;8 hook variants per winning body.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Body (middle 10&ndash;40 seconds).</strong> Impact: 15&ndash;25% of total performance. Once you have a winning hook, test different body structures: testimonial vs demo vs problem-solution, feature vs benefit messaging, long vs short format. Measure hold rate (what percentage reach the CTA) and click-through rate.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>CTA (last 3&ndash;5 seconds).</strong> Impact: 5&ndash;10% of total performance. Test CTA copy (urgency vs curiosity vs value), visual treatment (text overlay vs verbal vs button), and offer framing. Measure conversion rate specifically &mdash; CTR from CTA view to landing page click.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Format &amp; production style.</strong> Impact: 5&ndash;15% of total performance. Test talking head vs voiceover, vertical vs square, <a href="/ugc-video-ads/" class="text-accent hover:underline">UGC style</a> vs polished, static vs motion. Format tests are broad &mdash; they inform your overall creative strategy rather than optimizing a single ad.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Why this order matters</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">If you test CTAs before hooks, you&rsquo;re optimizing the last 5 seconds of an ad that 70% of people never see. The math doesn&rsquo;t work. Optimize for the widest funnel first (hook = everyone who sees the ad), then narrow (body = people who watched past the hook), then narrow further (CTA = people who watched the whole thing). Each layer of optimization multiplies the impact of the layers below it.</p>

        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-6 mb-6">
          <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-3"><strong>Example:</strong> Improving your hook rate from 25% to 35% means 40% more people see your body and CTA. That single improvement cascades through the entire funnel &mdash; more views, more clicks, more conversions, lower CPA &mdash; without changing anything else about the ad.</p>
          <div class="bg-bg rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-[14px] text-ink text-center">
            25% hook rate &times; 10% CTR = <strong>2.5% overall</strong><br/>
            35% hook rate &times; 10% CTR = <strong>3.5% overall</strong> (+40%)
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 Sample Sizes & Statistical Significance -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="sample-sizes">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">04 &mdash; Statistical Significance</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Sample sizes and when to call a winner</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Most brands declare winners too early, leading to false positives</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Minimum: 50 conversions per variant for 80% confidence, 100+ for 95%</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">For top-of-funnel metrics (hook rate, CTR): 5,000+ impressions per variant</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The biggest mistake in creative testing is calling winners too early. You launch two ads, one has a 15% lower CPA after 24 hours and $200 in spend, and you declare it the winner. But with that sample size, there&rsquo;s a 40&ndash;50% chance the &ldquo;winner&rdquo; is actually worse &mdash; you just got lucky with a small sample. This is the same reason a coin can land heads 7 out of 10 times without being rigged.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Minimum sample sizes by metric</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Metric</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Min per variant (80%)</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Min per variant (95%)</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Typical time</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Hook rate (3s views)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">5,000 impressions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">10,000 impressions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">1&ndash;3 days</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CTR (click-through rate)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">5,000 impressions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">15,000 impressions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">2&ndash;4 days</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CPA (cost per acquisition)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">50 conversions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">100 conversions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">3&ndash;7 days</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">ROAS</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">75 conversions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">150 conversions</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">5&ndash;10 days</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The practical rule</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For most brands, the practical approach is: <strong>run each test for a minimum of 3&ndash;5 days with at least $50&ndash;$100 per variant per day</strong>. After 3 days, look at the data. If one variant is outperforming by 30%+ and you have 50+ conversions per variant, you can make a decision at 80% confidence. If the difference is under 20%, extend to 7 days to reach higher confidence. If the difference is under 10%, the variants are effectively equal &mdash; pick either and move on to testing a higher-impact variable.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The early kill rule</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Not every test needs to run to full statistical significance. If a variant has spent $200 and has zero conversions while the control has 10+, kill it. The math is clear enough. The early kill rule exists to prevent wasting budget on obvious losers. The threshold: if a variant is performing 50%+ worse than the control after reaching 50% of the minimum sample, you can kill it early with reasonable confidence.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05 Budget Allocation -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="budget">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">05 &mdash; Budget</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Budget allocation: testing vs scaling</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Allocate 20&ndash;30% of total ad spend to testing</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">70&ndash;80% goes to scaling proven winners</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Testing budget is an investment, not a cost &mdash; it produces tomorrow&rsquo;s winners</li>
