    <!-- 07 The Testing Workflow -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="workflow">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; Workflow</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">The creative testing workflow</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Every test starts with a hypothesis: &ldquo;Changing X will improve Y because Z&rdquo;</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Document everything &mdash; the learning log is more valuable than any single test</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Use the 60/30/10 production split: iterations, new angles, wild cards</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Step 1: Formulate the hypothesis</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Every test must start with a clear hypothesis. Not &ldquo;let&rsquo;s see if this works&rdquo; but &ldquo;we believe that a question-based hook will outperform a statement hook because our audience responds to curiosity.&rdquo; The hypothesis guides what you test, how you measure it, and what you learn regardless of the outcome. A test that disproves a hypothesis is just as valuable as one that proves it.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Step 2: Produce the variants</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Use the <strong>60/30/10 production split</strong> for each batch of test creatives:</p>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>60% iterations</strong> of proven winners (new hooks on winning bodies, fresh creators on winning scripts). Low risk, high probability of finding another winner.</li>
          <li><strong>30% new angles</strong> based on audience insights, competitor analysis, or customer feedback. Medium risk, medium probability. This is where you discover new winning concepts.</li>
          <li><strong>10% wild cards</strong> &mdash; experimental formats, unexpected angles, creative risks. Low probability of winning but occasionally produces breakout performers that redefine your entire strategy.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Step 3: Set up the test</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Use separate ad sets with identical targeting, budget, and optimization settings. The only difference should be the creative. On Meta, use the A/B test feature or manual CBO with equal ad set budgets. On TikTok, use the Split Test function. Equal conditions are essential &mdash; if one variant gets a bigger audience or budget, the test is invalid.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Step 4: Analyze and log</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">When the test reaches significance, log the result in a structured format: hypothesis, variants tested, results (with confidence level), learning, and action taken. This testing log becomes your most valuable asset. After 50+ logged tests, you will have a detailed map of what works for your audience that no competitor can replicate. This knowledge directly feeds your <a href="/performance-creative/" class="text-accent hover:underline">performance creative strategy</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Common Mistakes -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="mistakes">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Mistakes</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">The 8 most common creative testing mistakes</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Declaring winners too early is the #1 mistake (leads to 40%+ false positive rate)</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Testing too many variables at once makes results uninterpretable</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Not logging learnings means repeating the same failed experiments</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Declaring winners too early.</strong> Calling a test after $100 in spend and 5 conversions is not testing &mdash; it&rsquo;s coin-flipping. Wait for statistical significance (50+ conversions per variant minimum).</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Changing multiple variables at once.</strong> If you test a different hook, different creator, and different CTA simultaneously, you don&rsquo;t know which change caused the difference. Isolate variables or accept you&rsquo;re getting directional signals, not causal insights.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Testing CTAs before hooks.</strong> Optimizing the CTA of an ad with a bad hook is like polishing the bumper of a car with no engine. Fix the hook first.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>No testing budget allocation.</strong> Spending 100% on scaling and 0% on testing means your pipeline runs dry when winners fatigue. Reserve 20&ndash;30% permanently for testing.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Not logging results.</strong> Running 100 tests and remembering the results of 5 is a waste. A structured testing log turns individual tests into compounding knowledge. Document every test: hypothesis, variants, results, learnings.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Unequal test conditions.</strong> Giving one variant a larger audience, higher budget, or different optimization event invalidates the test. All conditions must be identical except the variable being tested.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Testing only what&rsquo;s easy to produce.</strong> If you only test hook variants because they&rsquo;re cheap to produce, you miss format-level insights that could unlock an entirely new performance tier. The 60/30/10 split addresses this.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Ignoring losing tests.</strong> A losing variant teaches you just as much as a winning one &mdash; maybe more. Log why it lost. The pattern of losses often reveals audience preferences that pattern of wins misses.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 Advanced Tactics -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="advanced">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">09 &mdash; Advanced</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Advanced testing tactics</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Concept testing: validate messaging angles before investing in full production</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Cross-platform testing: a winner on TikTok may or may not win on Meta</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Audience-creative interaction: different segments respond to different creative</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Concept testing with minimum viable creative</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Before investing in full UGC production, test the concept with minimum viable creative: a simple text-on-screen video, a static image ad, or a quick selfie-style video. If the concept (the messaging angle, the value proposition, the hook idea) doesn&rsquo;t resonate in its simplest form, polished production won&rsquo;t save it. This approach lets you test 5&ndash;10 concepts in the time and budget it takes to produce 1&ndash;2 polished videos.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Cross-platform creative testing</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A winning creative on TikTok might fail on Meta and vice versa. Audience expectations, content consumption patterns, and platform algorithms all differ. The concept (the underlying message) usually transfers, but the execution must be adapted. Test concepts across platforms, but always with platform-native execution. A TikTok-native talking-head video won&rsquo;t perform the same in a Meta feed.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Audience-creative interaction testing</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Advanced brands test not just which creative wins overall, but which creative wins for which audience segment. A testimonial ad might outperform a demo ad with cold audiences but underperform with retargeting audiences. Understanding these interactions lets you serve the right creative to the right audience at the right funnel stage &mdash; a significant competitive advantage.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The testing knowledge base</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">After 6&ndash;12 months of disciplined testing, your testing log becomes a strategic asset. Common entries include: &ldquo;Question hooks outperform statement hooks by 22% for cold audiences,&rdquo; &ldquo;Female creators outperform male creators by 15% for our product,&rdquo; &ldquo;Problem-agitate-solve body structure outperforms feature-list by 30%.&rdquo; This knowledge base makes every future creative better, reduces testing waste, and creates a compounding advantage competitors can&rsquo;t easily replicate.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

