        {[
          { q: "What is a creative testing framework?", a: "A creative testing framework is a systematic methodology for testing ad creatives to find winners faster, build audience insights, and reduce wasted ad spend. It includes testing methodologies (isolation, multivariate, sequential), a testing hierarchy (hook → body → CTA → format), sample size requirements, budget allocation rules, and a structured workflow for running and documenting tests." },
          { q: "How much budget should I allocate to creative testing?", a: "Allocate 20-30% of your total ad spend to testing. At $10K/month, that's $3,000. At $50K/month, that's $12,500. At $100K+, you can reduce the percentage to 15-20% since the absolute dollar amount is large enough. This budget funds the discovery of your next winning creatives — it's an investment, not a cost." },
          { q: "How many conversions do I need before declaring a winner?", a: "For 80% statistical confidence, you need at least 50 conversions per variant. For 95% confidence, you need 100+ conversions per variant. For top-of-funnel metrics like hook rate and CTR, 5,000-15,000 impressions per variant is the minimum. Running 3-5 days with $50-$100 per variant per day typically provides enough data for a reliable decision." },
          { q: "What should I test first in my ads?", a: "Test the hook (first 1-3 seconds) first. It has the highest impact on performance — 60-80% of an ad's success depends on whether people stop scrolling to watch. After optimizing your hook, test the body (middle section), then the CTA (end), then format and production style. This hierarchy ensures you optimize the highest-impact elements before refining smaller details." },
          { q: "What is isolation testing vs multivariate testing?", a: "Isolation testing (A/B testing) changes one variable at a time — same ad with two different hooks, everything else identical. It gives you clear, causal insights but is slower. Multivariate testing changes multiple variables simultaneously across many variants (e.g., 4 hooks × 3 bodies = 12 variants). It's faster for exploration but requires more budget and makes it harder to attribute results to specific changes." },
          { q: "How often should I launch new test creatives?", a: "It depends on your spend level. At $10K-$50K/month: launch a new batch weekly (4-8 concepts). At $50K-$200K: launch bi-weekly with rolling tests (10-20 concepts per cycle). At $200K+: launch continuously with 20-40+ new concepts per week. Higher spend means faster fatigue, which requires faster creative replacement." },
